= AI agent =

== Overview ==
An '''AI agent''' is a contemporary AI-powered system designed to carry out goal-directed multi-step tasks by selecting and executing actions, often using environmental feedback.

Modern AI agents commonly combine one or more models with software components for planning, tools, memory or state, retrieval, execution, and monitoring.

The exact architecture and terminology are not standardized.

== Agent versus model ==
A model maps inputs to outputs.

An agent system additionally has some mechanism for choosing and carrying out actions over a task trajectory.

Thus:

<pre>
model ≠ agent
</pre>

A [[Large language model|large language model]] can be a component inside an agent without being identical to the whole agent.

== Typical loop ==
Many modern AI agents can be described approximately as:

<pre>
observe / receive goal
→ decide or plan
→ select action or tool
→ execute
→ observe result
→ update state
→ continue or stop
</pre>

Systems vary widely in how many of these stages are explicit.

== Components ==
Common components include:

* a learned model or policy;
* [[Planning (artificial intelligence)|planning]] or task decomposition;
* [[Tool use (artificial intelligence)|tool use]];
* [[Agent memory (artificial intelligence)|memory]] or persistent state;
* retrieval;
* permissions and action interfaces;
* evaluation/monitoring;
* human approval checkpoints.

None is universally mandatory.

== AI agent and intelligent agent ==
[[Intelligent agent]] is the broader classical abstraction.

“AI agent” in current usage usually refers to implemented AI systems, often foundation-model-based, that perform delegated work.

The overlap is substantial, but the terms come from different historical layers.

== Agentic AI ==
“Agentic AI” is currently used as a broader label for architectures and systems emphasizing agency, autonomy, multi-step action, adaptation, or multi-agent orchestration.

Its taxonomy is still evolving.

NIST's 2026 work treats agentic AI as a distinct standards, evaluation, interoperability, governance, and risk-management area.

== Autonomy ==
An AI agent can require approval before sensitive actions and still be an agent.

Full independence is not required.

[[Autonomous agent (artificial intelligence)]] describes higher operational autonomy.

